<BaseLayout
  title="About AttoLang"
  description="The motivations behind AttoLang and the Oresoftware Dev House building it."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <p class="kicker">About</p>
    <h1>Stateful applications deserve a better language.</h1>
    <p>AttoLang is designed for long-lived, actor-based software where state, concurrency, ownership, hot upgrades, and isolation work together—not just stateless REST endpoints.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="page-body">
    <div class="prose">
      <h3>Why we started AttoLang</h3>
      <p>
        AttoLang was motivated by the need to create more <strong>stateful applications</strong>,
        rather than treating everything as a stateless REST API. WebSocket servers, gaming backends,
        interactive UIs, state machines for distributed coordination, message queues, and development
        servers all depend on long-lived state, reliable messaging, and safe evolution while running.
      </p>
      <p>
        Modern runtimes also host many independent jobs inside one machine: application plugins,
        serverless functions, actors, background tasks, and user-authored code. Process isolation can
        be expensive; unrestricted shared memory can be dangerous. AttoLang explores a compiler-and-VM
        approach to explicit isolation, actor ownership, and controlled capabilities.
      </p>

      <h3>Erlang's resilience, Rust's safety, Orleans-style state</h3>
      <p>
        AttoLang draws on <strong>Erlang</strong> for messaging, supervision, and fault-tolerant actors;
        <strong>Rust</strong> for static typing, ownership, and memory-safety principles; and
        <strong>Microsoft Orleans</strong> for its approach to stateful, actor-oriented applications.
        The aim is a next-generation language and runtime that makes distributed state and
        concurrency easier to reason about without sacrificing low-level control.
      </p>
      <p>
        The goal is not merely memory safety. The language model should reject accidental ambient
        authority, dangling references, unsafe actor messages, and mutable data races before
        they become production failures—and keep the safe path ergonomic.
      </p>

      <h3>Performance and deployment ambitions</h3>
      <p>
        The performance target is <strong>more than 10× Erlang's speed with roughly one-tenth the
        compute</strong> on equivalent, reproducible workloads. This is an <strong>aspiration,
        not an established benchmark result</strong>. The <a href="/performance/">performance page</a>
        tracks measurement methodology and evidence.
      </p>
      <p>
        AttoLang is pursuing full ahead-of-time (AOT) compilation, native binaries for target
        platforms, zero tracing garbage collection in LLVM-native execution, and Rust-class
        type and memory safety. Its actor runtime is designed for fault tolerance and
        generation-fenced hot upgrades with rollback. These are architectural goals;
        <a href="/language/">feature status</a> and backend-specific verification remain important.
      </p>

      <h3>About the name</h3>
      <p>
        In Italian, <em>atto</em> means an act or deed: action. In Japanese,
        <em>attō</em> (圧倒) conveys overwhelming or overpowering. Together those ideas
        express the name's intent: action with the ability to handle demanding systems.
      </p>
      <p>
        AttoLang was formerly known as Oreslang. Some GitHub repository names, source-file
        extensions and compatibility identifiers still use the original name while the
        project migrates. These historical names do not change the current AttoLang identity.
      </p>

      <h3>Oresoftware Dev House</h3>
      <p>
        AttoLang is authored by <a href="https://github.com/oresoftware">Oresoftware Dev House</a>,
        with the compiler and related projects collected under the
        <a href="https://github.com/ores-truffle-oreslang">ores-truffle-oreslang GitHub organization</a>.
      </p>
